#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "67da2ffa-b472-5084-985c-dde4cd2df661" :question/math-academy-id "e-5219" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/89500561af6affb81c697e1bffe36957.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$, and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) + ((y - (-1))^{2})/(3^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(25) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(9) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "e01914e2-b4f0-52b5-baf1-8b7fa5da3f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-5222" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/0de79119b131777f87a4e2fdd6f3a262.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$, and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - (-1))^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y - (-3))^{2})/(4^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x + 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "10dda4fa-e047-53f4-a20a-b881e14f3e2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5223" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1$, find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2$, so the center is located at $(2,-2)$.\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16$.\n\n- Since $a^2=25$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n- Since $b^2=16$, the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4$.\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5$, and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4$.\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length\n\n$$\n2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10\n$$\n\nand the minor axis has length\n\n$$\n2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8\n$$"}]}
